dts: rk3576: clock the GPU from SCMI, with per-variant OPPs

The GPU OPP table lists 700, 800, 900 and 950 MHz, but panfrost drives
CLK_GPU, a divider off GPLL/CPLL/AUPLL/SPLL/LPLL. None of those produce
those rates, so the clock framework rounds down and the hardware silently
runs slower than the OPP says. Measured on an ArmSoM Sige5, 700 MHz runs
at
594 and 800, 900 and 950 all collapse onto 786.

Rates above the PVTPLL threshold are only reachable through SCMI: BL31
owns
the GPU PVTPLL and switches between the normal PLL and PVTPLL on an SCMI
rate
request. Make SCMI_CLK_GPU the clock panfrost scales, with PCLK_GPU_ROOT
as
"bus" and CRU CLK_GPU as "gate". BL31 programs PVTPLL through registers in
the GPU block reached over those two and does not enable them itself;
without
the gate named here nothing claims it, so the clock framework disables it
as
unused during boot and the first rate request wedges the CPU.

The per-variant OPP selection lands in the same commit rather than a later
one. The S, J and M parts stop at 800 MHz, so any commit that reaches the
upper rates without the restriction in place would offer them a rate they
are
not rated for. Panfrost's generic speed-bin path passes the nvmem value
straight to dev_pm_opp_set_supported_hw(), but Rockchip's cell holds a
part
number, so a per-compatible callback translates it. An unreadable cell
selects
a restricted table rather than the base part's, because the failure mode
of
guessing is a rate and a voltage the silicon was not qualified for.

specification-serial-number@8 moves from the Sige5 board dts up into
rk3576.dtsi next to the other OTP data cells: the GPU node lives in the
SoC
dtsi, so a label defined only by one board's dts would not resolve when
any
other RK3576 board is built.

The base part's voltages become the highest the vendor lists for each
rate.
The vendor picks one of eleven per-OPP voltages from a runtime PVTPLL
measurement, which is not reimplemented here, so the table has to be safe
for
the slowest silicon rather than tuned to one die.

Drop the 950 MHz OPP. BL31's rate table has no 950 MHz entry, so the
request
is rejected outright, and table 3-2 of the datasheet (rev 1.5) gives 900
MHz
as the GPU maximum.

The driver parks the clock explicitly before the power domain goes down,
so
there is no opp-suspend entry here: it would only overwrite the rate the
driver is tracking, and the vendor kernel does not use one on this SoC.

Signed-off-by: Isaiah DeRose-Wilson <isaiahdw@gmail.com>

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/panfrost/panfrost_devfreq.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/panfrost/panfrost_devfreq.c
@@ -182,11 +182,90 @@
